window.docContents[82].resolve({"/rules/RG1007/#RG1007-established":{"contents":"The registration is closed, names an eligible executable constant, and Lean checked a proof of the stated predicate about it.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That R expresses the intended behavior (R-INTENT) and that every caller uses the contracted implementation (R-INVARIANT). Every accepted account lists these as open for each reported contract.* Behavior of compiled code beyond the Lean definition; execution boundaries are RG3001 and RG3002.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-INTENT: the proposition expresses the intended requirement, with its quantifiers, hypotheses and limits* R-INVARIANT: the intended invariants, write paths and callers are all covered by proof-bearing interfaces* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG1007: Executable contracts require supported closed evidence","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG1007/#RG1007-established"},"/rules/RG4004/#RG4004-established":{"contents":"The teaching fence elaborated warning-free and contains an authenticated compiler-trusting declaration.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* Any conformance of the teaching example; it is classified, never counted as a positive proof.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG4004: Teaching examples require authenticated compiler classification","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG4004/#RG4004-established"},"/rules/RG5003/#RG5003-established":{"contents":"Every registered public material declaration's docstring has a nonempty labelled Intent section, decided by the proved classifier RegulaPolicy.materialDocumentationFailure.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the intent states what the requirement owner needs, or that the declaration meets it (R-INTENT, R-DOC). There is no intent detector, length threshold or similarity check.* Code fences in the docstring are not tracked: a # Intent line inside a fenced block counts as an Intent heading.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-INTENT: the proposition expresses the intended requirement, with its quantifiers, hypotheses and limits* R-DOC: documentation is complete and faithful to the declarations and requirements\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG5003: Registered public material declarations require an Intent section","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG5003/#RG5003-established"}});